<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Kotor</h1><p class="st-meta">1371 CE – 1420 CE · capital Kotor</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Self-governing Adriatic port of Kotor between the fall of Serbian rule and Venetian annexation, trading under shifting Hungarian and Bosnian protection.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kotor, a Romance-speaking port at the head of its deep bay, had long enjoyed self-government under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a>. As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire</a> disintegrated, the city accepted the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> in 1371 and ran its own affairs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of nobles governed the commune under its own statutes, with a Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathedral-of-saint-tryphon">cathedral of Saint Tryphon</a> at its centre. Kotor lived from sea trade and from carrying goods to and from the Balkan interior, rivalling <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Ragusa</a>. Its protectors changed often: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia">Tvrtko</a> I of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a> from 1385, and pressure from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zeta-under-the-balsici">Zeta under the Balšići</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Facing threats from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšići</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, Kotor placed itself under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1420. It remained Venetian until 1797, and its walls and old town are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1371 CE – 1420 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.99%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1371 CE – 1420 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kotor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dalmatian; Latin; Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kotor, Cattaro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=commune-of-kotor" aria-label="Commune of Kotor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5135 669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Kotor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5455 925L5521 939M5455 925L5446 783M5455 925L5437 871M5455 925L5293 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5521 939h0M5446 783h0M5437 871h0M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5455 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5455 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kotor. <a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=commune-of-kotor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
